// The compiler's own `abla_darwin_fork` (#811), for the code it JIT-compiles (`ablac run`, the REPL, compile-time
// slices): the runtime's Darwin dispatcher calls it for fork 57 (stdlib/abla/runtime/platform/host.ab), and a module
// ORC builds without optimization keeps that reference even on a host where the macOS-only call can never run, so
// the compiler defines it on every host. On macOS it is libSystem's fork, the one the dispatcher needs; elsewhere
// nothing calls it and it answers ENOSYS (a constant, so the compiler imports no libc fork: #806). A macOS program
// gets the C adapter's (runtime/abla_darwin_compat.c), which is weak there so that this one is the compiler's. The
// link keeps it exported (src/toolchain.ab: -Wl,-u,_abla_darwin_fork on macOS).
private noescape extern:"c" fun fork(): i32
private noescape extern:"intrinsic" fun ablaHostIsMacOS(): bool

@export("abla_darwin_fork")
fun bootstrapDarwinForkExport(): int = if (ablaHostIsMacOS()) int(fork()) else -38
